1. CHECK THE SOURCE AND HOST
Use an isolated checkout of https://github.com/danaia/ANGI.git. Fetch
{{sourceRef}}, check out FETCH_HEAD detached, and record git rev-parse HEAD.
When the source ref is a full commit ID, require HEAD to equal it. Keep that
commit throughout the install. If the ref, installer, board, or guide is missing,
stop. Run python3 scripts/detect-board.py --require {{board}}. If experimental,
explain what still needs hardware verification and ask before proceeding.
Use a Linux build environment on this computer. On macOS use a local Linux VM.
On Windows use WSL 2 or a local Linux VM. ARM64 Linux builds its own toolchain
and may take longer. Confirm the Linux environment can see
the real whole device or dedicated network interface. If it cannot, stop and
help me choose a local Linux environment with that access. Do not guess.
{{access}}

2. BUILD AND CHECK ANGI
Run python3 scripts/install-source.py prepare --board {{board}} {{prepareArgs}}
using an existing private output directory outside the checkout. This command
records the commit, preflights the host tools, RAM, capacity and transport,
selects the workstation Ed25519 key, and runs:
  make configure BOARD={{board}}
  make dependencies BOARD={{board}}
  make build BOARD={{board}}
  make check-image BOARD={{board}}
It checks the board stamp and runs the equivalent of sha256sum -c
{{artifact}}.sha256, then makes a private copy with the public login key and a
fresh SSH host key. Verify the private artifact and known_hosts with:
  python3 scripts/provision-sd.py --verify PRIVATE_DIR --board {{board}}
Monitor the build until it finishes and report progress periodically. Stop on
a failed build, image check or SHA-256 mismatch, before requesting erase approval.
Record the board, commit, final artifact checksum and private trust path. Keep
the workstation private key and the private boot artifact out of chat and logs.

